selftest: this line was written through the Log capability
  ok   the Log capability writes to the kernel log
  ok   the Info capability describes tasks and their capabilities
  ok   only init holds a capability for the reset port 0x64
  ok   the drivers refuse to crash or hang for an ordinary program
  ok   recv can poll, and can give up after a time limit
  ok   a call that nobody receives times out and leaves nothing queued
  ok   a call to an endpoint that nobody can receive from gives E_DEAD
  ok   an endpoint has one receiver at a time
  ok   a call carries its label, words and badge; a reply carries no badge
  ok   a program cannot send the kernel's labels or an oversized message
  ok   a 256-byte message crosses a page boundary in both directions
  ok   a server can end in an orderly way, and its clients are told
  ok   a reply needs the token of the call, and the token works once
  ok   callers are served in the order in which they called
  ok   a caller is told when the server dies with its request
  ok   a caller that is killed while it waits leaves nothing queued
  ok   a reply to a caller that was killed gives E_INVAL
  ok   revoking a capability takes back its copies and theirs, and nothing else
  ok   revoking reaches another task: its copy and what it mapped through it
  ok   revoking wakes the tasks that waited through the copies
  ok   revoking an interrupt capability frees the line its holder had bound
  ok   a capability goes along with a call when both ends hold the grant right
  ok   without the grant right at both ends nothing is handed over
  ok   the sender takes back what it sent
  ok   a reply carries a capability back, still linked to where it came from
  ok   a waiting task that loses the grant right takes no more capabilities
  ok   a capability is not handed to a task whose table is full
  ok   memory of a device is refused as a message buffer, also in part
  ok   a device page taken from a waiting task was never its message buffer
  ok   a reply reaches a caller whose capability was revoked in between
  ok   map gives zeroed pages that can be written
  ok   a map that fails part-way changes nothing
  ok   map and unmap accept only whole pages inside user space
  ok   unmap gives every page frame back
  ok   a task cannot take more memory than its quota
  ok   shared memory keeps the rights and the bounds of its capability
  ok   a copy of a memory capability covers the same pages or fewer
  ok   a program cannot run code from its stack
  ok   a program cannot run code from its data
  ok   a program cannot run code from memory it mapped
  ok   the exit code of a child reaches its parent
  ok   a panic ends the program that panicked
  ok   reading address 4 is a page fault at address 4
  ok   a program cannot write to its own code
  ok   a program cannot read kernel memory
  ok   a program cannot write kernel memory
  ok   a program cannot jump into the kernel
  ok   a stack overflow ends the program with a page fault
  ok   a division by zero ends the program
  ok   an invalid instruction ends the program
  ok   a program cannot switch interrupts off
  ok   a program cannot halt the processor
  ok   a program cannot use I/O ports without a capability
  ok   floating-point instructions are refused
  ok   fourteen children ended, twelve by a fault, and left nothing behind
  ok   a task that never yields is pre-empted and can be killed, once
